{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>Complete the week of this one.</b> It is joined with a construct, in the accusative (חטף): \"the week of this one,\" and these are the seven days of the feast. This too is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 29:27). If so, why did the Rav not explain that \"the days were completed\" refers to both the labor and the condition, which were fulfilled according to the words of Onkelos (Onkelos on Genesis 29:27)? For that is truly the plain sense of the verse — and on account of the days that her mother mentioned, and also because of his old age, Laban would not give him his daughter before the time that the two of them had stipulated, and it is sufficient that he fulfill his stipulation. And according to the words of Onkelos, it is necessary to explain \"complete the week of this one\" as referring to the days of the feast, for the days of the labor were already complete when Jacob said so to him, and Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 29:27) explained likewise. But I do not know, for the seven days of the feast are an enactment of Moses our Teacher for Israel (Jerusalem Talmud, Ketubot 1:1). And perhaps one may say that the distinguished ones among the nations practiced this from the beginning, as in the case of mourning, as it is written (Genesis 50:10), \"and he made a mourning for his father seven days.\" And that which they derive here in the Jerusalem Talmud (Mo'ed Katan 1:7) and in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 70:19), that one does not mix one rejoicing with another rejoicing — this is merely support based on the customs of the ancients before the Torah. But in our Gemara (Mo'ed Katan 9a) they did not derive it from here; rather, they derived it from \"and Solomon made the feast\" (I Kings 8:65). And it is possible to say that this was in exchange for his wages of ten rams (Genesis 31:41), for Jacob said to him at first that the days were completed, and Laban was silent and gave him Leah; and afterward Laban said to him, \"Complete the week of this one,\" for the days of Leah's labor had not yet been completed — \"and before my appointed time I gave her to you\" — and Jacob listened to him and completed them according to Laban's words, for what could he do, and he longed for Rachel. Therefore the verse did not state at the outset, \"And it came to pass when the days were completed, Jacob said, etc.\" Furthermore, it is possible to say that when he was in the seventh year, Jacob said to Laban, \"Give me my wife, for my days are completed\" — this being the year of the completion of the days. And similarly, \"old and full of days\" (Jeremiah 6:11) means one who has reached the year of his end; and likewise, \"until the day of the completion of the days of your consecration\" (Leviticus 8:33) means until the seventh day on which the days of consecration are completed. Or he said \"were completed\" because they were near to being completed and reckoned as though completed, and there are many like this. And so in the other passage (Genesis 35:18), \"as her soul was departing, for she died\" — when she was close to that and reckoned as though she had died. And this is the meaning of \"and I will come to her\": that is, not that you should give her and I will go, but rather that I will marry her and complete the few days that are upon me, for from now on you need not fear that I will leave you. And our Sages made a midrash (Bereshit Rabbah 70:18) on the expression \"and I will come to her,\" because it is not the way of propriety to mention such a thing even regarding the righteous, but the intention is what I have said. And afterward Laban said to him: complete the years of Leah, this one, for perhaps because I acted against your wishes you will not complete them; or so that it would be known when the days of Rachel's labor began, and then I will give you the other one for the labor which you shall serve with me after the marriage." "<b>And she will be given to you.</b> This is plural language: \"let us go down and there let us confound\" (Genesis 11:7), \"and let it be burned\" (ibid. v. 3) — likewise this is the language of \"and He will give\"; this is what Rashi explained (Rashi on Genesis 29:27). And he did not say why the singular should be given plural form, though perhaps he considers it the way of the dignified in the holy tongue, as others have said (see Ibn Ezra above on Genesis 1:26). And Rabbi Abraham said here (ibid.) that it is in the niphal conjugation, and the vav converts it to the future, and its meaning is \"and she will be given to you.\" But it seems correct to me that Laban's words were spoken with cunning. He said to Jacob: \"It is not done so in our place, for the men of the place will not permit me to do so, for it is a disgrace among them; but you complete the week of this one, and I will give you — I and all the men of the place — also this one, for we all agree to the matter, and we will do you honor and make a feast as we did the first time.\""], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {0 "The term חטף here refers to the grammatical phenomenon where the noun in a construct chain takes a short (hurried/חטוף) form; Ramban notes that שְׁבוּעַ is in construct with זֹאת.", 1 "The plural references in Genesis 11:7 and 11:3 are first-person plural cohortative forms, not technically identical to the phenomenon in our verse; Ramban cites Rashi grouping them together under 'plural language.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:37:37.473287143Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}